  "content": {
    "id": "p38-q15-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "15",
    "prompt": "Chris and Sarah each roll a die three times. Find the probability that Chris obtains more sixes than Sarah.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\dfrac{6295}{23328}$.\n\nUse symmetry and subtract the probability that their numbers of sixes are equal.",
      "worked": "Let $C$ and $S$ be Chris’s and Sarah’s numbers of sixes. Both have binomial distribution with $n=3$ and $p=\\frac16$. Their probabilities for $0,1,2,3$ sixes are respectively $\\frac{125}{216},\\frac{75}{216},\\frac{15}{216},\\frac{1}{216}$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(C=S)&=\\left(\\frac{125}{216}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{75}{216}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{15}{216}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{1}{216}\\right)^2\\\\\\Pr(C=S)&=\\frac{5369}{11664}.\\end{align*}$$\nBy symmetry, $\\Pr(C>S)=\\Pr(S>C)$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(C>S)&=\\frac{1-\\Pr(C=S)}{2}\\\\\\Pr(C>S)&=\\frac{1-\\frac{5369}{11664}}{2}\\\\\\Pr(C>S)&=\\frac{6295}{23328}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 52
  },
